WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.380
The 2010s were a decade of slicing, dicing,
and monstrous thrills, and horror fans were

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.420
treated to a whole host of legitimately terrifying
films.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.220
From satanic thrillers to psychological suspense
flicks, these were the best horror movies

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.260
of the last decade.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.900
One of Darren Aronofsky's best films, Black
Swan is a fever dream that follows Nina, a

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.240
solid corp ballerina in an esteemed New York
ballet company with dreams of one day dancing

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:24.740
the lead.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.380
When she does finally get her big break, Nina
begins to crumble under the pressure to embody

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.780
perfection, and her already-tenuous grasp
on reality slips from her grip entirely.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.040
And that's where things start to get a little
bit freaky.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.080
In reference to Nina's penchant toward self
harm, as well as the film's body horror, The

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:41.580
Guardian writes:

00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:47.600
"Fantastically deranged at all times, Darren
Aronofsky's ballet psycho-melodrama is a glittering,

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.220
crackling, outrageously pickable scab of a
film."

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.960
In a rare event for a horror movie, Black
Swan went on to sweep during awards season.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:59.460
And lead actress Natalie Portman walked away
with the Oscar, BAFTA, Critic's Choice, and

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:04.080
SAG Award wins for her nuanced portrayal of
a mentally ill artist teetering on the brink

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.909
of sanity until her eventual and terrifying
fall.

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:11.900
Arguably one of the most self-aware horror
movies since Wes Craven's Scream, The Cabin

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:16.281
in the Woods follows a group of gorgeous youngsters
and their one goofy stoner friend during a

00:01:16.281 --> 00:01:19.640
long weekend at a cabin in the woods owned
by a distant relative.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.560
But as The Sydney Morning Herald's Jake Wilson
put it:

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.420
"It sounds like the oldest horror story in
the book but from the first scene of The Cabin

00:01:26.420 --> 00:01:30.560
in the Woods, it's clear that the director,
Drew Goddard, and his co-writer, Joss Whedon,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.900
are bent on turning the formula upside down."

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:33.920
"The hair dye."

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:35.980
"Dumb blonde, very artistic."

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:39.020
"Works its way into the blood, through the
scalp.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:40.760
Very gradual."

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.080
Things get incredibly bloody and funny, and
the movie works as a genuine horror flick,

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.580
a comedy, and a fantastic critique of the
slasher genre.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.020
Exquisite Terror's Naila Scargill wrote:

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.320
"Much will be lost on those less accustomed
to the classic horror film, but this is not

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.719
to say it won’t be enjoyable; in fact it’s
a perfect introduction to the genre, accessible

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:00.880
enough to reel folk in."

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:05.799
When twin brothers Elias and Lukas' mother
comes home to their quiet lake house after

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:10.000
cosmetic surgery on her face, the boys begin
to suspect this woman isn't actually who she

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.100
says she is at all.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:16.600
Goodnight Mommy quickly takes a dark and grotesque
spiral into madness as the boys test the woman

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:20.140
in horrifying ways, trying to prove that she
is or isn't their mother.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.920
Needless to say, this movie is pretty rough.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.220
As Lenika Cruz of The Atlantic writes:

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.880
"Beneath it all is the nagging and heartbreaking
feeling that these boys have lost their protector.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.280
That vulnerability, combined with their isolation,
their confusion, can be harder to stomach

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:35.940
than some of the more graphic scenes."

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.940
North Shore News' Julie Crawford agrees, saying:

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:43.160
"Ripped straight from the pages of your therapist's
notebook, Goodnight Mommy is a thoroughly

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.820
terrifying fairy tale with a killer twist."

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:51.220
After one of the most terrifying opening scenes
in horror history, we're introduced to Jay,

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.860
a young girl who has an uncomfortable romantic
tryst with a dude named Hugh.

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:59.340
The affair results in Jay becoming the vehicle
for a monstrous creature that will kill her

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.950
and the person it haunted before her if she
doesn't pass it on to someone else through

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:03.880
intimate contact.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:09.260
The shape can only be seen by the person infected,
making this a gritty film not just about relationship

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:13.600
paranoia, but also the old-fashioned kind
of paranoia where Jay questions her very hold

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:14.540
on her sanity.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.100
"This thing.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.100
It's gonna follow you."

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:25.940
Further, It Follows' backdrop of a crumbling
Detroit adds yet another level of social commentary

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.820
about cultural disintegration, along with
mental and physical degradation.

00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:31.860
BuzzFeed's Alison Willmore asks:

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:36.600
"It's a testament to how scary a movie It
Follows is that for days after watching it,

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:40.730
you walk around thinking up survival plans
should you hide, stay on the move forever,

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:42.320
pass the haunting on to someone else?"

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.060
Is there a third option?

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:45.460
Not get infected at all?

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:46.920
Let's pick that one instead.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:51.920
Fede Álvarez's taut invasion horror with
a twist, Don't Breathe features three young

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:55.690
thieves who decide to rob a blind man rumored
to keep hundreds of thousands of dollars in

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:56.800
cash in his house.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.760
Little do these inept robbers know, the homeowner
is ex-military, and his blindness hasn't affected

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.420
his physical prowess or tactical abilities
in any way, shape, or form.

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:06.660
The New Yorker writes:

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:11.040
"The suspense is built as carefully as it
is in a good John Carpenter movie; Alvarez

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:15.360
uses the camera like a stealth weapon, exploring
dark corners and hidden areas of the house

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:16.600
with devilish glee."

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.040
Father Son Holy Gore agrees, saying:

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:22.919
"There's so much to enjoy, even when we're
taken down the rabbit hole of depravity after

00:04:22.920 --> 00:04:24.900
the secrets of the house are finally revealed."

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.960
The premise of Train to Busan is pretty straightforward.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:31.760
A father and his estranged young daughter
get on the train from Seoul to Busan, where

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.400
they come to find out a zombie virus has been
unleashed.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:38.880
But this is more than just a zombie movie,
even for all its gore and violent action as

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:40.420
the humans struggle to survive.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.520
Written and directed by Yeon Sang-ho, the
story is a character study of a man and his

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:55.830
fractured relationship with his daughter and
ex-wife.

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:59.720
It also manages to create three-dimensional
characters quickly within the other humans

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:04.500
battling for and mostly losing their lives,
which only makes the story even more wrenching.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.270
RogerEbert.com' Brian Tallerico raves:

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.820
"The most purely entertaining zombie film
in some time, finding echoes of George Romero's

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:16.150
and Danny Boyle's work, but delivering something
unique for an era in which kindness to others

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:17.590
seems more essential than ever."

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:23.310
In rural New England, decades before the Salem
Witch Trials and subsequent panic that continues

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:27.449
to haunt the region, a farmer and his family
are forced to relocate to an even more remote

00:05:27.449 --> 00:05:31.920
area after unwarranted suspicions about them
threatens full-out banishment by the church.

00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:36.430
Soon enough, the community's distrust of the
family seems to be justified as it turns out

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:39.400
there really is something evil lurking in
the forest.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:44.400
A grim installment of the folk horror sub-genre,
The Witch is a truly scary film that deals

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:46.020
with far more than just the devil.

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:47.640
And according to The Dispatch:

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:51.320
"Its masterful blending of the sacred and
the blasphemous  makes it not

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:55.660
just a great horror film, but perhaps the
first true horror masterpiece of the new century."

00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:57.980
And as The Guardian's Benjamin Lee says:

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:02.460
"Like any outstanding horror film, its true
impact only reveals itself once the credits

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:06.180
have rolled and it stays buried under your
skin, breaking through every now and then

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.900
to remind you of its insidious power."

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:13.309
While Jordan Peele's seminal Get Out was far
from the first important installment of black

00:06:13.309 --> 00:06:17.270
horror, it was one of the first to garner
mainstream attention that hasn't abated even

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.100
slightly in the years since its release.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:23.760
This quietly menacing horror movie centers
on a young black man as he discovers the nefarious

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:27.740
nature of his white girlfriend's family when
he shows up at their secluded home.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:32.330
Get Out isn't just a parable about the underlying
racism that seeps into all corners of American

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:35.939
society, including supposedly progressive
and liberal circles.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:40.740
"By the way, I would've voted for Obama for
a third term if I could've.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.680
Best president in my lifetime, hands down."

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.560
It's also a scathing indictment of modern-day
slavery as pertains to the commodification

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:49.700
of black bodies in America in particular.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.220
For Cosmopolitan, Kendra James writes:

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.260
"In using both realities in his movie, Peele
brings Get Out to a higher level of horror,

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:59.380
at least for any person of color in the audience.

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:01.900
We're all keenly aware of how possible it
is."

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:04.440
No teacups allowed while watching this one,
though.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:09.740
Trey Edward Schult's sophomore feature It
Comes at Night takes place in a post-apocalyptic

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:13.680
world, but don't expect a film like Zombieland
or Mad Max: Fury Road.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:18.740
Instead of zany laughs or wild action, this
is a grim and atmospheric horror drama that

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:22.839
features a family doing its best to survive
the end days after a deadly disease wipes

00:07:22.840 --> 00:07:23.940
out most of the world.

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.020
Here, paranoia is as contagious as the deadly
infection, and the surviving family living

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:33.420
in a remote cabin does what they need to do
in order to survive... no matter what.

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:38.479
The movie features powerhouse performances
from its small cast, and as Karl Delossantos

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:40.020
of Smash Cut Reviews notes:

00:07:40.300 --> 00:07:42.419
"This is a morality play at its finest.

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:43.960
There are no heroes and no villains.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:45.960
There are simply humans in a house."

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.219
Even though Andy Muschietti's adaptation of
Stephen King's killer clown opus It diverges

00:07:51.219 --> 00:07:55.199
wildly from the book in some problematic ways,
that didn't stop the movie from becoming the

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.480
highest-grossing horror film of all time.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:01.180
After all, in spite of its differences, the
film is pretty awesome.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:05.820
It follows seven youngsters who call themselves
the Losers' Club as they navigate not only

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:10.849
the perils of childhood, trauma, school bullies,
and abusive parents, but also a shapeshifting

00:08:10.849 --> 00:08:15.500
alien monster named Pennywise, who eats children
and has moved the seven Losers to the top

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:16.200
of his dinner list.

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:17.320
"Time to float."

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.979
While fans of the still-scary 1990 It miniseries
were unsure how Bill Skarsgård could take

00:08:23.979 --> 00:08:29.080
on Tim Curry's iconic performance as the murderous
Pennywise, Skarsgård's old-world European

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:32.920
spin on the character has become one of the
most terrifying monsters put to screen.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:34.760
Cultured Vultures writes:

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.480
"It provides a worthy adaptation to one of
King's best works.

00:08:38.680 --> 00:08:43.040
It's scary as all hell, but has enough heart
to keep even the most horror-averse audiences

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:43.680
engaged."

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:48.900
After the death of her overbearing mother,
Annie Graham's grief and anger begin to spiral

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:51.820
out of control in Ari Aster's debut film,
Hereditary.

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:55.840
It doesn't help matters any that her relationship
with her son Peter and her daughter Charlie

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:57.240
aren't exactly the best.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:01.540
And it's that rage and depression that prevents
her from noticing that something is very,

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:04.020
very wrong with her kids until it's far too
late.

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:09.220
And as the family dynamic begins to unravel,
Annie begins to realize there might be supernatural

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:10.080
forces at play.

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.280
The result is a movie that's almost too disturbing
to finish.

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:16.000
About this atmospheric film, Newsweek writes:

00:09:16.340 --> 00:09:20.460
"Hereditary feels like an endless drawing
out of that queasy, shocking, falling dream

00:09:20.460 --> 00:09:24.570
sensation, as the ground beneath the Graham
family, and the viewer, crumbles."

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:28.940
Toni Collette's nuanced and three-dimensional
performance of a mother past the edge in particular

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:30.870
received accolade upon accolade.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:34.380
Chicago Reader calls her, quote, "flawless,"
and The Times UK writes:

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:39.040
"It's an astonishing performance from Collette,
a disorientating cocktail of humane, hurtful

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:40.040
and hysterical."

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:45.420
In a surprising twist worthy of a horror movie
itself, The Office alum and comedian John

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:49.540
Krasinski wrote, directed, and starred in
the family apocalypse horror drama A Quiet

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:52.080
Place, alongside his wife Emily Blunt.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:09:56.020
In the film, the Abbott family is doing their
best to survive in a world that's been ravaged

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:58.320
by aliens that kill anything that makes a
sound.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.740
The film opens with the grotesque snatching
of one of the Abbott children, and it eventually

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:06.220
veers into body horror territory as Blunt's
Evelyn gets pregnant.

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:07.660
Slate says of the film:

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:11.980
"There are moments when the movie takes us
firmly by the hand and escorts us down a darkened

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:16.290
path, and they lead to one of the most profound
of communal pleasures: the sound of a movie

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:17.700
audience screaming as one."

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:22.250
In a movie filled with jump scares that its
cast can't vocalize, the only quiet place

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:23.240
is on-screen.

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:28.140
One of the few global truths women are taught
is to avoid walking alone at night whenever

00:10:28.140 --> 00:10:28.760
possible.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:33.280
Ana Lily Amirpour takes that truth and spins
it on its head in A Girl Walks Home Alone

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:37.500
at Night with her chador-and-Chucks-wearing
skateboard-riding antihero, the Girl.

00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:41.500
She walks whenever and wherever she wants
to... because she's a vampire.

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:46.880
The film's logline, "the first Iranian vampire
western," only partly encompasses the genre-bending

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:52.110
magic of the film, making it both as iconic
as original vampire stories like Nosferatu

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:55.260
while simultaneously reinventing vampire movies
altogether.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:10:57.440
Alexa Dalby of Dog and Wolf raved:

00:10:57.640 --> 00:11:00.420
"It has visual echoes of early Jarmusch and
Lynch.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:05.080
It's stunning to look at, slow paced, moody
and haunting an original feminist reinterpretation

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:08.260
of vampire mythology and its gender politics."

